<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="GBrowser: Settings reference" id="GBrowser-Settings-and-Configuration">

    <primary-label ref="gbrowser"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Settings: <ui-path>Settings | Tools | GBrowser</ui-path></p>
        <p>From the browser: <ui-path>More Options | Preferences</ui-path></p>
        <p>Scope: current project, stored in <path>.idea/gbrowsers.xml</path></p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Reference for every GBrowser setting: home page, search, history, theme, debug port, tool window, bookmarks, request headers and browser compatibility mode.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Set the home page, search, theme, request headers and compatibility mode.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Reference for the GBrowser settings in JetBrains IDEs: home page, search and history, page theme, debug port, request headers and compatibility mode.</web-summary>

    <p>
        GBrowser settings apply to the current project.
        Each project can have its own home page, bookmarks, history and request headers.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Open the GBrowser settings" id="gbrowser_settings">
        <procedure title="Open the settings page" id="open-settings" type="choices">
            <step>
                Open the <control>Settings</control> dialog (<shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+S</shortcut>, macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ,</shortcut>)
                and select <ui-path>Tools | GBrowser</ui-path>.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the GBrowser tool window, click <control>More Options</control> and select <control>Preferences</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            When you click <control>Apply</control> or <control>OK</control> on this page, GBrowser reloads the pages in all open GBrowser tabs of the project.
            Selecting a <control>Theme</control> option reloads them right away.
        </p>
        <p>The page has these groups, from top to bottom:</p>
        <list columns="2">
            <li><a anchor="default-home-page">Default Home Page URL</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="search-settings">Search Settings</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="appearance">Appearance</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="developer-options">Developer Options</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="toolwindow-settings">Tool Window Settings</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="bookmarks-management">Bookmark Management</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="http-response-headers">HTTP Response Headers</a></li>
            <li><a anchor="browser-compatibility-mode">Browser Compatibility Mode</a></li>
        </list>
        <!-- planned-image src="gbrowser_settings_overview.png" alt="GBrowser settings page with the home page URL, Search Settings and Appearance groups" -->
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Default home page" id="default-home-page">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Default Home Page URL" id="default-home-page-url">
                The page that opens in the first tab, in every new tab, and when you click <control>Home</control>.
                The default is <code>https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/14458-gbrowser</code>.
                The field cannot be empty.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <tip>
            Set the home page to the address of your local development server, for example <code>http://localhost:8080/</code>.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Search settings" id="search-settings">
        <p>The <control>Configuration</control> options control the address field and its suggestion list.</p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Highlight URL host" id="highlight-url-host">
                Saved with the settings, but GBrowser 2026.3.1 does not use it: the address field shows the URL without highlighting.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Highlight suggestion query" id="highlight-suggestion-query">
                Highlights the text you typed in the suggestion list.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Hide URL protocol" id="hide-url-protocol">
                Saved with the settings, but GBrowser 2026.3.1 does not use it: the address field always shows the full URL, including the protocol.
                The suggestion list hides the protocol regardless of this option.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Load favicon in popup" id="load-favicon-in-popup">
                Shows site icons next to the entries in the suggestion list.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Enable suggestion search" id="enable-suggestion-search">
                Adds Google search suggestions to the suggestion list, and opens a Google search when you enter text that is not a URL.
                When this option is on, the text you type in the address field is sent to Google.
                Off by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Life Span in new tab" id="life-span-in-new-tab">
                Controls where links open when a page opens them in a new window, for example links with <code>target="_blank"</code>.
                When the option is on, which is the default, such links open in a separate pop-up window.
                Clear it to open them in a new GBrowser tab instead.
                In version 2026.3.1, the comment under the option describes the reverse.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>The <control>Browser History</control> options control the history used for suggestions.</p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="History enabled" id="enable-browser-history">
                Saves the pages you visit for the suggestion list.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Number of history items to persist" id="amount-of-history">
                The number field next to <control>History enabled</control> sets how many pages GBrowser keeps, from 0 to 60.
                To stop saving history, clear <control>History enabled</control> instead of setting the number to 0.
                The default is 20.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <!-- planned-image src="gbrowser_settings_search_history.png" alt="Search Settings group of the GBrowser settings with the Configuration and Browser History options" -->
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Appearance" id="appearance">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Theme" id="theme">
                Sets the color scheme that GBrowser asks pages to use: <control>Follow IDE</control>, <control>Light</control> or <control>Dark</control>.
                Sites that support light and dark color schemes follow this setting.
                With <control>Follow IDE</control>, the default, open pages switch when you change the IDE theme.
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Developer options" id="developer-options">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Debug port" id="debug-port">
                Select the checkbox to edit the remote debugging port of the IDE's embedded Chromium browser in the number field next to it.
                The checkbox is off by default, and it only unlocks the field: clearing it does not change the port.
                The field shows 9222 unless the IDE already uses another port.
                The port applies to the whole IDE, not only to GBrowser, and a change takes effect after you restart the IDE.
                For how to use it, see <a href="GBrowser-DevTools-and-Device-Emulation.topic" anchor="remote-debugging"/>.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            To inspect a page from inside the IDE, you do not need a port: use <control>Open DevTools</control> (<shortcut>F12</shortcut>).
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="gbrowser_settings_appearance_dev.png" alt="Appearance and Developer Options groups of the GBrowser settings with the Theme options and the Debug port field" -->
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Tool window settings" id="toolwindow-settings">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Hide toolwindow label" id="hide-tool-window-label">
                Hides the <control>GBrowser</control> title in the tool window header, which leaves more room for tabs.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Show tab icon" id="show-tab-icon">
                Shows the site icon on each tab.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Show bookmarks toolbar" id="show-bookmarks-toolbar">
                Shows the bookmarks bar under the address field.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Enable tab splitting" id="enable-drag-and-drop-tabs">
                Lets you drag a tab to an edge of the tool window to show two tabs side by side.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Reload previous tabs" id="reload-previous-tabs">
                Reopens the saved tabs when you reopen the project.
                GBrowser saves the first five tabs when you close the project.
                Off by default.
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Bookmark management" id="bookmarks-management">
        <p>
            The <control>Bookmark Management</control> group is collapsed by default.
            It contains a table of your bookmarks with <control>Name</control> and <control>URL</control> columns.
            The <control>Bookmark Manager</control> action (<shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+B</shortcut>) opens this page with the group expanded.
            For details, see <a href="GBrowser-Bookmarks-and-History.topic" anchor="edit-bookmarks"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Request headers" id="http-response-headers">
        <p>
            The <control>HTTP Response Headers</control> group defines headers that GBrowser adds to its requests.
            Despite the group name, these are request headers, for example an authorization token or a feature flag for your development server.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Add a request header" id="add-request-header">
            <step>
                Expand <control>HTTP Response Headers</control> and click the add button above the <control>Headers</control> table.
            </step>
            <step>
                In <control>URI Regex</control>, enter a regular expression that must match the whole request URL, for example <code>http://localhost:8080/.*</code>.
                Use <code>.*</code> to match every URL.
            </step>
            <step>
                Enter the header <control>Name</control> and <control>Value</control>.
                <!-- planned-image src="gbrowser_settings_request_headers.png" alt="HTTP Response Headers group of the GBrowser settings with a header rule for a localhost URL" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                Optional: select <control>Overwrite</control> to replace the header when the request already has it.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            The table starts with a <code>User-Agent</code> row whose <control>URI Regex</control> is empty, so it does not match any request.
            To stop sending a header, clear its <control>URI Regex</control> cell and click <control>OK</control>.
            To also delete a stored value, such as a token, clear its <control>Value</control> cell.
            In version 2026.3.1, the remove button does not delete a header: GBrowser keeps sending it, and the row is back the next time you open the settings.
        </p>
        <warning>
            Header values are stored in plain text in <path>.idea/gbrowsers.xml</path>.
            Do not commit that file if it contains tokens or passwords.
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Browser compatibility mode" id="browser-compatibility-mode">
        <p>
            Some sites block browsers that are embedded in other applications or that look automated.
            For <code>perplexity.ai</code>, <code>challenges.cloudflare.com</code>, <code>openai.com</code>, <code>chat.openai.com</code> and <code>claude.ai</code>,
            GBrowser always sends the User-Agent of a regular desktop browser, whatever the settings below.
        </p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Enable browser compatibility mode" id="enable-compatibility-mode">
                When this option is on, GBrowser runs a script that hides automation markers when a new tab's browser is created on one of the domains in <control>Compatible sites</control>.
                On by default.
            </def>
            <def title="Compatible sites" id="compatible-sites">
                The domains that the compatibility script applies to.
                Adding a domain does not change the User-Agent that GBrowser sends to it.
                The defaults are <code>perplexity.ai</code>, <code>challenges.cloudflare.com</code>, <code>openai.com</code>, <code>chat.openai.com</code> and <code>claude.ai</code>.
                To change a domain, select it and click the edit button; to remove a domain, select it and click the remove button.
                Changes to the list are saved right away, even if you then click <control>Cancel</control>.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <procedure title="Add a site" id="add-compatible-site">
            <step>
                Expand <control>Browser Compatibility Mode</control> and click the add button under <control>Compatible sites</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>Add Anti-Detection Site</control> dialog, enter the domain, for example <code>example.com</code>, and click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Open the site in a new GBrowser tab.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <!-- planned-image src="gbrowser_settings_compatibility.png" alt="Browser Compatibility Mode group of the GBrowser settings with the list of compatible sites" -->
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where GBrowser stores its data" id="storage">
        <p>
            GBrowser stores its settings, bookmarks, history, request headers and compatible sites in <path>.idea/gbrowsers.xml</path> in the project directory.
            The tabs saved for <control>Reload previous tabs</control> are in <path>.idea/gbrowser_project.xml</path>.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you share the <path>.idea</path> directory through version control, these files are shared too.
            Add them to your ignore file if you want to keep your bookmarks, history and headers private.
        </p>
    </chapter>

